{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi2mrLDwjiDvWHHzjNAc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**BASKETBLAM** is finished: over-the-top 2-on-2 arcade basketball. No fouls. No mercy. All BLAM.\n\n- **The game:** `Basketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-061027.html` in this folder's root. It's one self-contained file that runs from `file://` and inside the AI Hub.\n- **Dev files:** in `Basketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-061027/`.\n- **Tests:** `node tests/run.mjs` runs everything: 1,070 checks, 0 failures. That includes a scripted playthrough of the whole Tour and all four AI Hub harness scenarios.\n\n**What's in it**\n- **Core play:** turbo, a dribble synced to the ball, jump shots with a release meter, layups, 12 dunks, alley-oops and putbacks. Defense has steals, legal shoves, spin moves, blocks and goaltending, plus rebounds.\n- **Big moments:** three baskets in a row ignites SUPERNOVA. Dunks can shatter the backboard, which cuts to a sponsor break.\n- **Match extras:** six power-ups, the CPU AI on four difficulty levels, and juice with substitutions.\n- **Rules:** quarters, overtime twice, then SUDDEN BLAM (the next basket wins).\n- **Courts and teams:** 5 courts, each with its own twist (Jackpot Spot, sea breeze, oil slicks, eruptions with steam vents, a robot boss). 6 teams with 18 players, plus story characters.\n- **Modes:**\n  - **Blam Tour:** four games with story scenes, an ending, credits and unlocks.\n  - **Exhibition:** one to four players on one machine.\n  - **Blam Camp:** 10 lessons.\n  - **Also:** Hall of Blam, How to Play, and About/Credits with the report pages.\n  - **Hidden extras:** seven cheat codes.\n- **Attract mode:** starts after 10 seconds idle on the title or any menu screen. It plays five scripted demo segments that show every feature, and any input leaves it.\n- **Dev mode:** from the pause menu. It has live sliders for every tuning value with reset (one or all), plus cheats, match controls, scene jumps, debug overlays, and time and camera controls.\n- **Controls and screens:** works with a controller, keyboard or mouse alone. Prompts show PlayStation symbols by default, with an Xbox option in Settings.\n- **Touch-only devices:** get \"Controller or keyboard required\" with a Settings button, and the attract mode still runs.\n- **Online:** a shared world with our own card, where the host runs the match. Players who leave turn into CPU players, and time-up and server mode are handled.\n- **Audio:** all music, sound effects and gibberish voices are made in code; there are no audio files.\n\n**What was scaled back from the plan**\n- There's no shoot-around physics bench.\n- The courts use the engine's props rather than the ~15 custom shapes the plan listed.\n- A few smaller set pieces from the plan aren't there, such as the Dome's upper deck.\n\n**Balance:** scoring runs higher than the plan's target of 30–60 points per team. In full-length CPU-vs-CPU games, teams scored 50–90 points each, and about 44% of baskets were dunks.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 06:10:27\n- **Source:** Basketball, in the over-the-top arcade style (genre inspiration; an original game with original teams, courts and characters)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports, 2-on-2 basketball with a short story ladder. Side-on broadcast camera (orthographic, pitch 30°, panning with the ball, with a dunk cam); optional High (three-quarter) camera.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (modified), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript, Canvas 2D, Web Audio. Tests on Node 24 (`node:vm`, headless) and Playwright 1.63 with Chromium; Python http.server for the hub harness.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native HD pixel style (PS1-era 320×240, expanded to fill the screen), pushed toward a 90s arcade broadcast: dressed rigs in jerseys, shorts and socks, hand-painted court floors, a backboard turned toward the camera, a spring net, crowds, backdrops, lighting and comic banners.\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural Humanoid rigs, plus a new pose layer over `solvePose`. It moves the hands by IK for the dribble, shots, 12 dunk routines, blocks, steals, shoves, spins, hanging on the rim, celebrations and taunts.\n- **Audio:** 12 songs and 7 stings with dynamic layers, about 50 sound effects made in code, an Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish voice synth with 20+ voice profiles, and a pink-noise crowd. No audio files.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared world, 1–4 players. Our own card; the host runs the simulation; clients send input 15 times a second and receive interpolated snapshots, with their own player predicted. Leavers turn CPU; time-up and server mode are handled.\n- **Testing:** Headless scripts using `game.advance` and `game.debug`, behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count; this includes a scripted playthrough of the whole Tour. Also Playwright screenshots and the AI Hub harness scenarios.\n- **Research:** None beyond the engine handoff docs, the sports kit and the AI Hub protocol README.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown (no usage figures are available inside the session)\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **Issues caught:** about 18 in the game itself, plus about 10 mistakes in the tests. Game examples:\n   - A shot released at the arena wall started outside the wall and lost half its speed.\n   - A pass receiver wandered off instead of meeting the ball.\n   - Rebounds after a rim touch weren't counted.\n   - Scene changes stalled while dev time was frozen.\n   - Camp handed the ball to the interns after a basket.\n   - Dunks made up 77% of baskets until rebalanced.\n   - Several layout overlaps on screens.\n2. **Critical:** 3.\n   - The canvas never filled the window.\n   - The custom broadcast camera wasn't registered, so the game fell back to another view.\n   - Freezing time in dev mode would have stalled every scene change.\n3. **Most helpful:** the scripted playthrough of the whole Tour. It drives the real menus with a bot playing the human seat through the real input actions, loses a stop on purpose and replays it, and reaches the Hall of Blam. The first CPU-vs-CPU smoke match was a close second.\n4. **Least helpful:** re-taking screenshots after small layout tweaks. Each cost much more time than a script and found nothing that one look at a single image wouldn't.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **Liked most:** headless determinism (`game.advance` plus `game.debug`), and HD Humanoid rigs that look good with no art files.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong:** a custom `E.View` has to be registered in `E.VIEWS` before `setView` can find it, and the page has to size the canvas with CSS.\n3. **Built myself:** the basketball pose layer (IK hands, a dribble synced to the ball, dunk keyframes) on top of `solvePose`.\n4. **Hardest part:** getting the hoop's layers (glass, rim halves, net halves, ball) to draw in the right order in an orthographic side view.\n5. **Changed in the engine:**\n   - Basketball shorts and socks on the Humanoid.\n   - Afro, flattop and mohawk hairstyles.\n   - The dialog's voice can be a function called for each letter (for the gibberish voices).\n   - Public audio buses, so my voice and crowd sounds follow the volume settings.\n6. **One change I'd make:** built-in support for posing a rig's hands with IK, so games don't have to wrap `solvePose`.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **Liked most:** the complete, testable definition of done (dev mode, attract mode, hub, 10-foot UI, the report), and the freedom over genre, camera and stack. The plan made every decision concrete.\n2. **Confusing:** the plan was written for another model and timestamp, so its file names had to be remapped to this TASK.md. The plan is also far larger than one medium-effort session, so it was unclear how much of it was expected.\n3. **Hardest:** fitting the full feature set (story ladder, ten lessons, a five-segment attract director, a dev panel, shared-world netcode) into one session while keeping it tested.\n4. **Would change:** say how much of a large plan is in scope at a given effort level, and give the plan file names that match the TASK.md it's used with.\n\n`exit-interview.json` is beside the HTML and parses as JSON. The one empty value is `aiHub.series`, which TASK.md allows. The same report text appears on the in-game About screen."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":611,"cache_read_input_tokens":614331,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":611},"output_tokens":821,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"10afac7a-fac6-483a-81de-891e67f83b42","uuid":"cfc7b42e-6752-42c5-b0f0-a07a3123d3ae","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:17:57.087Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi2mqvurnLhrcS6abjKo"}
